blurb: The South Portland Board of Education discussed updates on the athletic complex
  and middle school field, reviewed current enrollment trends, and detailed the implementation
  plan for the new personal electronic device policy. The meeting also highlighted
  a significant grant received for student homelessness prevention.
votes: []
summary:
- topic: South Portland High School Athletic Complex Update
  text: The South Portland High School Athletic Complex project is progressing on
    time and within budget, with groundbreaking anticipated for May 2027 and completion
    by September 2027, pending permitting. Design details for track and field events,
    environmental lighting, and new restroom facilities were finalized, and the board
    inquired about the contractor selection process and organic field maintenance.
- topic: South Portland Middle School Field Readiness
  text: The South Portland Middle School Field is ready for use for school-based activities
    and middle school sports this fall, following successful aeration, overseeding,
    and fertilization. The administration recommended limiting its use to internal
    school activities this fall to allow the grass to fully establish before opening
    it up for external tournaments in the spring.
- topic: Enrollment Trends and Class Sizes
  text: Overall enrollment shows a slight increase of 20 students compared to last
    year, though elementary enrollment exhibits a general declining trend. Kindergarten
    enrollment is approximately 200 students, and the administration is making adjustments
    to address class sizes, including adding a teacher at Small Elementary, while
    monitoring the impact of these changes.
- topic: Implementation of Personal Electronic Devices Policy
  text: The board reviewed the implementation plan for the new state-mandated 'bell-to-bell'
    ban on personal electronic devices, noting different approaches for elementary,
    middle, and high schools. The high school will implement a 'locked in a way' system,
    and the administration outlined required exceptions, progressive responses to
    violations, and a plan for ongoing monitoring and review.
- topic: MaineHousing Student Homelessness Prevention Program Award
  text: South Portland received a $40,000 award from the MaineHousing Student Homelessness
    Prevention Program, making it one of seven districts to receive this competitive
    grant. This funding is crucial for providing direct aid to unhoused students and
    families for critical needs such as rent and utilities, managed by the McKinney-Vento
    liaison.
- topic: Staff Resignations
  text: Several staff resignations were reported, including Brianna DeRice, an Instructional
    Support Teacher at South Portland High School; Tyler Pelletier, a Humanities Teacher
    at South Portland Middle School; Allison Hopkins, Principal of Dyer Elementary;
    and Bethany Connolly, Principal of Skillin Elementary.
- topic: Elementary Reconfiguration Update
  text: An update was provided on the Elementary Reconfiguration Steering Committee's
    work, including findings from a recent workshop that engaged approximately 30
    participants. This indicates ongoing efforts to plan for elementary school changes.
