  desc: Superintendent Atkinson Duina introduced the implementation plan for the new
    state-mandated policy on personal electronic devices, framing it within a broader
    societal discussion about technology use. She emphasized the need for intentional
    technology use and support for managing devices.
  children:
  - seconds: 4278
    speaker: Atkinson Duina
    text: Superintendent Atkinson Duina detailed how the personal electronic device
      policy would be implemented across different grade levels. Elementary students
      are advised to keep devices at home or have them locked in the main office,
      middle school students must have devices off and locked in lockers, and high
      school students will have devices collected and stored in lock boxes each morning.
  - seconds: 4485
    speaker: Atkinson Duina
    text: Superintendent Atkinson Duina outlined the state-mandated exceptions to
      the personal electronic device policy, including uses consistent with IEPs or
      504 plans, medical and health plans (e.g., glucose monitors), language support
      for emergent English learners, and emergency authorizations by administrators.
      She clarified that these exceptions are determined on an individual basis.
  - seconds: 4643
    speaker: Atkinson Duina
    text: Superintendent Atkinson Duina explained the district's approach to enforcing
      the new policy, emphasizing consistent communication, a progressive response
      to violations aligned with PBIS, and family notification. She also highlighted
      the commitment to monitoring the policy's effectiveness and conducting a two-year
      review cycle, with an initial update to the board planned for November.
